  "content": {
    "id": "p59-q32-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "32",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p59-q32-stem",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Discriminant of the depressed cubic. ",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ],
              "colour": "#8b5cc7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "P(x)=x^3-px+q",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", where ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "p>0",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", and assume that ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\cos3\\theta=4\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos\\theta",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p59-q32-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "By using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ for the right value of $r$, show that solving $P(x)=0$ reduces to solving\n$$\\cos3\\theta=-\\frac{3q}{2p}\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{p}}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$r=2\\sqrt{\\frac p3}$, so $x=2\\sqrt{\\frac p3}\\cos\\theta$ and $\\cos3\\theta=-\\frac{3q}{2p}\\sqrt{\\frac3p}$.\n\nChoose $r^2=\\frac{4p}{3}$ to match the supplied triple-angle identity.",
          "worked": "Substitute $x=r\\cos\\theta$, choosing $r>0$ with $r^2=\\frac{4p}{3}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(r\\cos\\theta)&=r^3\\cos^3\\theta-pr\\cos\\theta+q\\\\P(r\\cos\\theta)&=\\frac{pr}{3}(4\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos\\theta)+q\\\\P(r\\cos\\theta)&=\\frac{pr}{3}\\cos3\\theta+q.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $pr>0$, $P(x)=0$ is equivalent to\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos3\\theta&=-\\frac{3q}{pr}\\\\\\cos3\\theta&=-\\frac{3q}{2p}\\sqrt{\\frac3p}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p59-q32-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that if $P(x)$ has three real zeroes, counting multiplicity, then $4p^3-27q^2\\ge0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$4p^3-27q^2\\ge0$.\n\nThree real roots allow the substitution in part (a); apply $|\\cos3\\theta|\\le1$ and square.",
          "worked": "Let the three real roots be $\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma$. The root relationships give $\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma=0$ and $\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha\\gamma+\\beta\\gamma=-p$. Thus\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\beta-\\gamma)^2&=(\\beta+\\gamma)^2-4\\beta\\gamma\\\\(\\beta-\\gamma)^2&=\\alpha^2-4(\\alpha^2-p)\\\\(\\beta-\\gamma)^2&=4p-3\\alpha^2\\ge0.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $|\\alpha|\\le2\\sqrt{\\frac{p}{3}}$, so write $\\alpha=2\\sqrt{\\frac{p}{3}}\\cos\\theta$ for a real $\\theta$. Part (a) and the range of cosine give\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left|\\frac{3q}{2p}\\sqrt{\\frac3p}\\right|&\\le1\\\\\\frac{27q^2}{4p^3}&\\le1\\\\4p^3-27q^2&\\ge0.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 86
      }
    ]
  },
